WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.879 --> 00:00:06.760
Throughout history people have tried weird
and wonderful ways to stay healthy.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:12.720
The Aussies said that sitting inside a dead
whale carcass would cure rheumatism; the Ancient

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:19.619
Egyptians used alligator dung as a contraceptive
(yes, really); the Victorians encouraged people

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:23.160
to eat tapeworm eggs to aid weight loss.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:28.890
The Romans whitened their teeth with urine
– morning breath is bad enough, never mind

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.409
piss breath.

00:00:30.409 --> 00:00:35.530
Yet as long as there has been physical illness,
there has also been mental illness.

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:40.980
And as long as there has been mental illness,
there has been a variety of highly disturbing,

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:47.630
so-called ‘treatments’ that make Harold
Shipman seem about as deadly as a damp flannel.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:53.620
In the 1800s field of psychology began to
emerge and the idea that the brain should

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.870
be treated differently than the body.

00:00:56.870 --> 00:01:03.340
Psychology soon diverged into three main schools
of thought: phrenology, psycho-physics, and

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:04.600
moral therapy.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:09.799
Now phrenology is a since-discredited field
which basically involves studying lumps and

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:16.020
bumps on the skull to try and predict a person’s
personality or character traits.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.649
Scientists figured that the brain was like
a muscle, and therefore parts that were ‘exercised’

00:01:21.649 --> 00:01:28.219
more would grow more, and so bumps were a
sign of well-developed brain tissue.

00:01:28.219 --> 00:01:32.959
Based on that logic Jay Leno should be the
smartest man alive.

00:01:32.959 --> 00:01:36.909
Phrenologists would map the skull into different
sections, each responsible for a different

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:41.399
attribute - for example, the bit above the
ear could tell you how self-destructive a

00:01:41.399 --> 00:01:46.219
person was, and the very top of the crown
was linked with confidence levels.

00:01:46.219 --> 00:01:52.619
In fact, the phrase “you should get your
head examined” comes from this practice.

00:01:52.619 --> 00:01:57.319
The problem was that scientists still had
a pretty poor understanding of what parts

00:01:57.319 --> 00:02:02.810
of the brain controlled different aspects
of behaviour, and so the theory was dismissed

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:03.969
by the mid 1800s.

00:02:03.969 --> 00:02:10.550
I mean, just look at this phrenological mental
map, it looks like a paint-by-numbers of Gwentyth-Paltrow’s

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.810
bullshit marketing terminology.

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:17.400
By then, scientists had moved onto psycho-physics,
which wanted to explore the link between the

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.380
physical world and mental experience - for
example, how does light make something bright?

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:26.400
Or how does sound make something loud?

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:32.000
Wilhelm Wundt – who has the most german
name since Volkswagen – is nicknamed the

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.670
‘father of psychology,’ by those who aren’t
so fond of Frued and incest.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:43.080
Wundt was a pioneer in this field - he would
collect data by recording patient’s responses

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.180
to different stimuli, for example showing
them different colours.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:52.540
Wundt and his contemporaries assumed that
if you could control the conditions in a laboratory,

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:54.820
then you could control a patient’s reactions.

00:02:54.820 --> 00:03:01.820
This problem is, experiences are subjective
- for example, it’s presumed not everyone

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:06.710
sees colours as the same hue – your red
could be my blue.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:14.140
And take music – Beethoven’s 9th fills
me with wonder and serenity but it turns Alex

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:19.140
DeLarge into a quivering, feeble fetus.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.500
Psycho-physics taught us a lot about how we
experience things, but didn’t really tell

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:27.410
us why people experience things differently.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:33.190
Lastly, the Victorians practiced moral therapy,
the belief that people with mental disorders

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:38.300
could be cured by offerings of compassion,
kindness and dignity.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:43.530
Various religious groups, including the Quakers,
argued that the asylum system needed to be

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:49.410
reformed so it was more sympathetic towards
patients, who should not be seen as raving

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:55.770
animals, and chained up, as was typical, but
vulnerable human beings.

00:03:55.770 --> 00:04:01.460
Part of reforming the system involved redesigning
the buildings in which patients were housed;

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.520
this is the period where all those asylum
hospitals you see in movies like One Flew

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:09.990
Over The Cuckoo’s Nest and Shutter Island
were built - imagine grand buildings with

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:13.240
gardens where patients could get some fresh
air.

00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:19.959
In America Dr Thomas Story Kirkbride advocated
the use of architecture as part of the cure

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:23.650
and not just a prison for the afflicted.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:29.280
So he designed a blueprint for the ‘perfect
asylum’ - one that would enable patients

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:35.680
to be classified by type of illness but also
enable easy surveillance, with ample sunlight

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:37.620
and ventilation.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:46.600
Between 1845 and 1910, 73 Kirkbride plan hospitals
were built, and many still exist today.

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:50.840
Now moral therapy, which we mentioned earlier,
advocated that these institutions needed to

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:57.380
offer a safe space for vulnerable people,
and should only be used as a short-term solution.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:04.260
But in reality, budget constraints, overcrowding,
poorly trained staff and dubious psychiatric

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:09.190
knowledge resulted in a very different system.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:15.680
Most asylums functioned as glorified prisons,
a place to keep individuals with mental illnesses

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:21.139
away from their families and communities,
because they were seen as a threat.

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:28.640
The problem was anyone who exhibited ‘abnormal
behaviour’ was deemed a lunatic, and, as

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:35.750
anyone who’s ever endured reality television
will tell you, the term normal is rather subjective,

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:38.970
leaving the system wide-open to abuse.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:44.110
For example, many inmates were simply political
prisoners, people who’d expressed radical

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:49.660
views and so were conveniently dismissed as
mad and locked up.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:54.580
Women were particularly vulnerable to misdiagnosis
and were institutionalised much more frequently

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.580
than men.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:06:02.180
At the time a woman’s assets automatically
passed to her husband upon death or incarceration,

00:06:02.180 --> 00:06:08.020
and so lunatic asylums offered a nifty way
to get rid of an unwanted spouse and make

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:09.150
a quick bit of cash.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:16.009
The law also stated that any man in charge
of a woman - so a father, husband or brother

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:22.050
- could have a woman detained against her
will, and many women who spoke out against

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:28.130
female oppression were deemed hysterical and
dangerous by those closest to them.

00:06:28.130 --> 00:06:34.680
In 1887, a pioneering journalist Nellie Bly
heard about the mistreatment of female inmates

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:41.050
at an insane asylum in New York, and decided
to go undercover, by acting insane to have

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:47.020
herself committed, to find out if the rumours
were true – the following year Bly would

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:53.420
recreate Phileas Fogg’s fictional trip around
the world in a record breaking 72 days, so

00:06:53.420 --> 00:06:58.020
it’s fair to say she was prepared to go
the distance.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:04.320
In the asylum she discovered conditions more
cruel and inhumane than she could have ever

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:06.760
imagined.

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:11.100
Patients were forced to consume spoiled food
and contaminated water.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:16.260
They were tied together and forced to pull
carts around like mules.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:20.990
Sometimes they were made to sit on hard, wooden
benches for up to 12 hours a day, and if they

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.300
moved, or made a noise, they would be beaten.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:31.120
Throughout the nights she heard constant screams
harrowing down the long, empty corridors.

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:36.400
One woman repeatedly begged for her death
whilst another bellowed ‘murder’ over

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:38.310
and over.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:43.350
Bly also saw one of the nurses beat a patient
with a broom-handle for crying.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:49.110
Then, they tied her up, covered her face with
a sheet and bound it tightly around her neck

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:55.070
to subdue her screams and placed her in a
bathtub filled with ice-cold water.

00:07:55.070 --> 00:08:00.370
Nurses and orderlies then held her in the
tub until she gave up struggling and lost

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:02.300
consciousness.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:08.080
What she witnessed was known as Hydrotherapy
– a common ‘treatment’ for patients

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.880
experiencing manic episodes.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:17.430
Doctors believed the shock of cold water would
help to reset patients’ systems.

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:22.900
People were sometimes submerged in a bath
for hours at a time, ‘mummified’ in cold

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:28.259
wet sheets, or handcuffed to a shower and
sprayed with icy water.

00:08:28.259 --> 00:08:33.240
The Victorians were so convinced about the
benefits of hydrotherapy they invented all

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:39.990
sorts of strange contraptions to give you
a good soaking at home; like this rocking

00:08:39.990 --> 00:08:46.760
bath tub, which was basically your very own
wave machine, or the ‘bath of surprise’,

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:52.870
which could dunk you into ice water at any
moment, and probably explains why most people

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.380
don’t like surprises.

00:08:54.380 --> 00:09:00.180
What shocked Nellie Bly the most was the fact
that most of the patients she met were not

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:05.010
suffering from mental illness before they
arrived at the asylum.

00:09:05.010 --> 00:09:12.620
But many had since experienced grave psychological
trauma because of their treatment.

00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:19.790
Now, Bly’s report did lead to an investigation
and improved conditions, but this barely scratched

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:22.750
the surface of what was going on behind closed
doors.

00:09:22.750 --> 00:09:28.001
Harriet Martineau, a British writer who is
known as the first female sociologist, wrote

00:09:28.001 --> 00:09:34.770
how in asylums, “we see chains and strait-waistcoats,
three or four half-naked creatures thrust

00:09:34.770 --> 00:09:40.200
into a chamber filled with straw, to exasperate
each other with their clamour and attempts

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:46.560
at violence, or else gibbering in idleness
or moping in solitude.”

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:49.370
Which does not sound like a fun Saturday night.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:54.810
When you think of insane asylums you might
think of padded rooms and straight jackets,

00:09:54.810 --> 00:09:59.650
but the Victorians were much more creative
than that.

00:09:59.650 --> 00:10:08.260
They used manacles, harnesses, coffins, coercion
chairs and crib beds to confine and subdue

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:09.270
patients.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:14.600
Some institutions forced patients to wear
Hannibal-Lector-style muzzles – but rather

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:20.290
than fava beans and a nice chianti, they got
freezing baths and a neck collar.

00:10:20.290 --> 00:10:25.290
Unsurprisingly, a very high number of people
died in asylums.

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:32.390
In 1829 William Scrivinger, a patient at Lincoln
Asylum, was found dead from strangulation

00:10:32.390 --> 00:10:39.020
after being strapped to his bed in a straitjacket
and left overnight without supervision.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:45.600
Doctors conducting the post-mortems discovered
a worrying trend in asylum deaths: patients

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:51.110
kept dying with broken bones, and broken ribs
in particular.

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:56.940
Many former patients wrote letters to authorities
about the abuses taking place, such as orderlies

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:02.029
subduing patients by kneeling on them until
they gave up.

00:11:02.029 --> 00:11:08.300
Eventually, an investigation in 1887, resulted
in a new legal precedent that asylum patients

00:11:08.300 --> 00:11:12.820
who died with broken ribs should be treated
as a manslaughter case.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:17.340
Subsequently, orderlies were taught to restrain
patients more humanely.

00:11:17.340 --> 00:11:22.830
Now given that people could be sectioned for
just about anything and everything, asylums

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:29.500
became overcrowded very quicklySo mental health
professionals – if you can call them that

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:33.940
– were under increasing pressure to come
up with ways to cure patients quickly, to

00:11:33.940 --> 00:11:39.440
have them dismissed and thus free up room
for the next unfortunate souls.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:45.839
This led to a lot of trial and error, which
was, well, mostly error.

00:11:45.839 --> 00:11:52.110
One of the most downright bizarre was rotational
therapy which posited that subjecting someone

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:57.870
to excessive centrifugal force should cure
their crazies.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:02.260
Patients were spun round and round at high
speed in a circular motion, normally in a

00:12:02.260 --> 00:12:08.230
swinging chair, until they eventually calmed
down and promised to obey the doctors.

00:12:08.230 --> 00:12:14.180
At this point they would be released and allowed
time to sleep and recover – as well as probably

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:16.780
vomit and change their trousers.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:22.589
The pioneer of this so-called ‘treatment’
was none other than Erasmus Darwin, Charles

00:12:22.589 --> 00:12:24.430
Darwin’s grandfather.

00:12:24.430 --> 00:12:30.940
Erasmus Darwin was a physician, philosopher
and scientist, but unfortunately he wasn’t

00:12:30.940 --> 00:12:36.149
particularly good at any of these, and so
wasn’t taken very seriously.

00:12:36.149 --> 00:12:42.149
Probably because he liked to record his ideas
in bad poetic verse like a lovesick teenager,

00:12:42.149 --> 00:12:47.491
but it also didn’t help that his ideas included
such gems as ‘excessive spinning to ease

00:12:47.491 --> 00:12:50.620
brain congestion.’

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:57.010
Nobody paid much attention to Darwin’s idea
at first, but then an American Doctor Benjamin

00:12:57.010 --> 00:13:02.890
Rush came along who thought spinning the ever-loving-shit
out of mentally-ill people sounded like a

00:13:02.890 --> 00:13:10.570
marvellous idea.Now, the aptly-named Benjamin
Rush claimed that rotational therapy had a

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:17.600
calming effect on his patients, but let’s
face it, strap anyone into a spinning teacup

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.550
for long enough, and they’ll probably pass
out and calm down eventually.

00:13:22.550 --> 00:13:28.970
If the ice baths and human salad spinner didn’t
cure the old noggin Victorian physicians usually

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.710
resorted to chemicals.

00:13:31.710 --> 00:13:36.640
In the form of tonics which contained an array
of powerful and addictive substances that

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:42.990
would make the FDA cry themselves to sleep,
such as morphine, lithium salts, cocaine and

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.120
chloroform.

00:13:44.120 --> 00:13:48.720
Furthermore, patients who were admitted with
syphilis, which was rampant at the turn of

00:13:48.720 --> 00:13:55.329
the 20th century, were injected with malaria
without their knowledge or consent, because

00:13:55.329 --> 00:14:02.760
it was supposed by Austrian doctor Julius
Wagner-Jauregg that malaria would kill syphilis

00:14:02.760 --> 00:14:03.760
in the bloodstream.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:11.050
To be fair, it did work to a certain extent
in reducing syphilis, the issue was about

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:17.380
15% of patients then died from Malaria – now
why does that not surprise me?

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:22.720
I would like to say that mental health treatments
improved after the Victorian era, but I’m

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.360
afraid it was quite the opposite.

00:14:25.360 --> 00:14:30.480
Electric shock therapy was widely practiced
in the early to mid 20th century.

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:38.370
And, in 1935, António Egas Moniz invented
the lobotomy, for which he was awarded the

00:14:38.370 --> 00:14:44.240
Nobel Prize in medicine but all it did was
turn people into human vegetables.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:51.280
By 1951 20,000 lobotomies had been carried
out in the US and other countries did their

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:52.860
fair share too.

00:14:52.860 --> 00:15:04.610
Funnily enough it was the USSR who were first
to outlaw lobotomies in 1950 because it was,

00:15:04.610 --> 00:15:09.200
in their words, ‘contrary to the principles
of humanity’.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:13.269
Europe and eventually the rest of the world
followed in subsequent years.

00:15:13.269 --> 00:15:18.709
And I think you’ll all join me in saying
thank goodness for that.

00:15:18.709 --> 00:15:24.390
But just to be safe, next time you visit your
psychologist and he offers you an iced tea,

00:15:24.390 --> 00:15:33.330
just take care and watch what he’s doing
with that ice pick.

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:39.570
Thanks for watching.

